# PAN-OS Tunnel Builder: Current Implementation Status

Status reviewed: 2026-08-21

## Overview

PAN-OS Tunnel Builder discovers, plans, and optionally loads an LSVPN-to-standard
IPsec migration into Panorama candidate configuration. It can identify the
spoke and hub firewalls and their source-owning templates, inspect an existing
LSVPN deployment, display a migration plan, save rollback and route snapshots,
and create the replacement candidate objects while removing satellite-side
LSVPN configuration.

Candidate changes require the explicit `build --load-candidate` option. The
default `build` workflow remains a local preview. The tool never commits
Panorama, pushes a template stack, or commits a managed firewall.

## Implemented CLI commands

### `panos-tunnel-builder status`

- Loads the Panorama connection settings.
- Connects through the PAN-OS Python SDK.
- Runs a Panorama system-information operational command.
- Reports successful connectivity and displays the returned system information.
- Separately reports PAN-OS API failures and unexpected/configuration errors.

### `panos-tunnel-builder build [FIREWALL]`

- Presents build output in operator-focused sections: Selection, Migration
  scope, Shared spoke configuration, Per-spoke tunnel plan, Notes, and Candidate
  load result. Common hub and crypto details are shown once, while each spoke
  lists only its endpoint, allocated link, routing behavior, routes, unique hub
  objects, and PSK-variable status.
- Omits template menus when both template numbers are supplied, deduplicates
  identical warnings across spokes, and avoids repeating final selections that
  were already shown at the beginning of the plan.
- Accepts a spoke firewall hostname or serial number, or prompts for it.
- Accepts a hub firewall with `--hub`/`-H`, or prompts for it.
- Rejects using the same managed firewall as both spoke and hub.
- Discovers the template stack assigned to each firewall.
- Lists every template in each stack and marks which templates contain network
  interfaces.
- Detects Ethernet, aggregate Ethernet, VLAN, loopback, and tunnel interfaces.
- Recommends the first interface-bearing template and allows an interactive
  selection, cancellation, or non-interactive selection by menu number.
- Rejects templates without interface configuration.
- Supports `--yes`/`-y` to skip confirmation prompts and accept a detected
  portal/active-gateway mismatch.
- Supports `--show-secrets` to display the generated pre-shared key; the default
  output hides it and displays only a short SHA-256 fingerprint.
- Accepts `--tunnel-address-pool` for LSVPN replacements, prompts for the pool
  when the option is omitted, and allocates one non-overlapping IPv4 `/30` or
  IPv6 `/127` point-to-point link per tunnel.
- Supports `--vsys` and `--hub-vsys` for selecting non-default spoke and hub
  virtual systems; both default to `vsys1`.
- Discovers the exact template that owns the LSVPN satellite, scans every stack
  that inherits that owner, and treats every affected managed firewall as one
  migration group. The initially selected interface template no longer defines
  migration scope. Multiple satellite-owner templates are rejected for separate
  group migrations.
- Refuses partial shared-template migrations. Every affected spoke must have a
  complete plan, the same satellite-object set, and a structurally compatible
  tunnel, zone, routing-instance, BGP, OSPF, and static-route layout.
- Creates one generic replacement IKE/IPsec/tunnel-interface structure in the
  shared spoke template. It allocates a `tunnel.N` that is unused on every
  affected spoke. Each hub object name contains the sanitized hub and spoke
  hostnames plus a deterministic three-digit suffix. Because Panorama entry
  names cannot vary through per-device template variables, the common
  spoke-side object names the hub and `shared-spokes`.
- Converts generated endpoint-specific tunnel addresses, IKE local and peer
  addresses, tunnel-monitor destinations, direct BGP addresses, static-route
  destinations, IKE identities, and PSKs to deterministic Panorama template
  variables. Each spoke receives device-specific overrides in its template
  stack; hub variables remain unique per spoke. Names describe their exact
  purpose, for example `spoke-tunnel-interface-address` and
  `hub-ike-pre-shared-key`, followed by a short deterministic identifier.
- Generates one PSK per spoke and writes the same value to its spoke override
  and matching hub variable. Non-secret companion fingerprints record the
  expected pairing, and deterministic ownership-marker variables prevent a
  same-name unrelated variable set from being adopted. Since Panorama does not
  expose PSK plaintext for comparison, a rerun rotates and rewrites both ends
  of every PSK pair in one transaction. Failed candidate attempts are rolled
  back and contribute no variables or credentials to the next run.
- Resolves each hub and spoke IKE local address against effective-running
  interface configuration. It selects the unique owning interface, maps the
  resolved address back to its raw Panorama template reference (including
  template variables), and blocks missing or ambiguous ownership before writes.
- Detects RFC1918, CGNAT, and local IPv6 spoke addresses and configures the hub
  for a dynamic peer with an exact, deterministic per-spoke IKE key ID encoded
  as a 64-character SHA-256 hexadecimal string accepted by PAN-OS KEYID fields.
- Supports `--load-candidate` to load the complete replacement into Panorama
  candidate configuration without committing or pushing.
- Rejects a dirty Panorama candidate by default so a failure cannot silently
  revert another administrator's pending work. `--allow-dirty-candidate`
  explicitly bypasses this safeguard and prints the full-candidate rollback
  warning.
- Before candidate writes, captures hub and spoke route tables under
  `--snapshot-directory`. Clean-candidate runs save a Panorama snapshot named
  `LSVPN_PRE_<id>_<timestamp>.xml`; explicitly allowed dirty-candidate runs use
  `LSVPN_CHK_<id>_<timestamp>.xml` to identify the file as a checkpoint that
  includes prior pending changes. Both obey PAN-OS's 32-character limit.
- Acquires a temporary Panorama configuration lock while preflighting and
  writing candidate changes, then releases it.
- Locates the exact templates that own the source satellite object, tunnel
  interface, routing membership, and zone membership instead of creating
  shadow objects in a selected but unrelated template.
- Writes and cleans each distinct network/routing/zone placement when multiple
  stacks share the satellite template but inherit different network templates.
- Removes the spoke satellite tunnel, old spoke tunnel interface, old
  zone/routing memberships, and directly bound spoke BGP/OSPF objects from
  candidate configuration after creating their replacements. Hub LSVPN portal,
  gateway, interface, and routing configuration is never removed.
- Refuses unrelated name and variable-type collisions. A rerun may update
  objects and variables carrying the tool's ownership markers and rotates both
  endpoints of each PSK pair.
- Treats a candidate-load rerun as a successful no-op when no LSVPN satellite
  configuration remains, without capturing routes or changing Panorama.

### `panos-tunnel-builder routes snapshot FIREWALL OUTPUT`

- Resolves a Panorama-managed firewall by hostname or serial.
- Discovers every legacy virtual router and every advanced-routing logical
  router/VRF from effective-running configuration.
- Retrieves IPv4 and IPv6 RIB entries through Panorama operational commands.
- Normalizes routing scope, prefix, protocol, administrative distance, metric,
  selected/installed state, and all ECMP next-hop/interface paths.
- Excludes volatile route uptime and internal implementation fields.
- Saves a versioned JSON artifact with firewall identity, virtual system, and a
  UTC capture timestamp. The same artifact includes effective virtual-router and
  logical-router configuration for post-commit drift validation.
- Refuses to overwrite an existing snapshot unless `--force` is supplied.

### `panos-tunnel-builder routes compare BEFORE AFTER`

- Requires both snapshots to represent the same firewall serial and virtual
  system.
- Reports missing, added, and changed route groups and exits nonzero when the
  normalized RIBs differ.
- Supports repeatable `--prefix-map`, `--interface-map`, and `--next-hop-map`
  `OLD=NEW` options. These account explicitly for the expected link subnet,
  tunnel interface, and next-hop changes during an LSVPN replacement without
  hiding unrelated route drift.

### `panos-tunnel-builder routes verify-migration MANIFEST`

- Consumes the non-secret validation manifest emitted by `build
  --load-candidate`, so route verification can occur in a separate run after
  operator-controlled commits, pushes, and routing convergence.
- Captures fresh hub and spoke route snapshots and compares them with their
  saved baselines using protocol- and path-specific interface/next-hop mappings.
- Allows only the expected replacement-link connected/local prefixes and
  temporary extra candidates for an otherwise preserved route; unrelated
  additions, removals, or changed routes still fail the command.
- Compares pre/post effective routing configuration after excluding only planned
  old/new tunnel-interface references and exact replaced static-route names.
  Unrelated BGP, OSPF, static-route, VR, or logical-router changes fail.

### `panos-tunnel-builder candidate restore SNAPSHOT`

- Loads a named Panorama configuration snapshot back into candidate
  configuration for rollback preparation.
- Requires confirmation unless `--yes` is supplied.
- Does not commit Panorama or push managed firewalls.

## Firewall and template discovery

- Resolves managed firewalls by case-insensitive hostname or serial number.
- Supports resolving a short hostname to a managed FQDN.
- Suggests close managed-firewall names when a requested name is not found.
- Detects empty, unknown, and ambiguous firewall identifiers.
- Detects missing, empty, or ambiguous template-stack assignments.
- Preserves the template order reported by the assigned stack.
- Treats only templates containing interface configuration as eligible targets.

## Existing LSVPN discovery

For every spoke inheriting the selected shared template, the tool reads
candidate configuration through Panorama and:

- Finds IPsec tunnels of type `global-protect-satellite` in the selected spoke
  virtual system.
- Captures the satellite tunnel name and tunnel interface.
- Captures the portal address, local interface, and local IPv4/IPv6 address.
- Captures published routes and the publish-connected-routes setting.
- When publish-all is enabled, derives eligible connected networks and static
  destinations from the satellite tunnel interface's exact virtual router or
  logical-router VRF. It excludes default routes and routes using the old LSVPN
  or satellite external interface.
- Captures certificate references, anti-replay, copy-TOS, and tunnel-monitor
  settings.
- Queries the firewall's operational state for its active GlobalProtect gateway,
  gateway address, and tunnel-monitor status.
- Converts operational-command failures into plan warnings so discovery can
  continue where possible.

For the selected hub, the tool:

- Reads effective GlobalProtect portal satellite gateway assignments.
- Falls back to the firewall's effective-running configuration when a targeted
  configuration API query omits portal settings inherited from Panorama
  templates.
- Uses the spoke's active gateway address or name first when multiple portal
  candidates exist, then prefers a spoke serial match when runtime state is not
  available. A serial match is not required because PAN-OS can select satellite
  configurations through enrollment user/group authentication. Treats a device
  value of `any` as unrestricted.
- Requires a single unambiguous gateway after runtime and serial matching;
  unresolved multiple gateways are rejected rather than guessed by priority.
- Warns when the portal-designated gateway differs from the spoke's active
  gateway and can request confirmation.
- Matches the portal gateway to effective GlobalProtect gateway configuration on
  the selected hub by name when possible, or by gateway address and tunnel
  interface when portal, gateway, and site-to-site object names differ.
- Treats the endpoint address as gateway identity when portal configurations use
  different labels for the same gateway, and explicitly rejects ambiguous
  address or tunnel-interface mappings.
- Supports portal gateway endpoints expressed as IPv4, IPv6, CIDR, or FQDN.
  FQDN matching is case-insensitive, accepts a trailing DNS root dot and IDNA
  names, and resolves DNS when the corresponding site-to-site object stores only
  a local IP address.
- Reads the hub gateway's external interface and IPv4/IPv6/floating addresses.
- Reads its LSVPN tunnel interface, interface IPs, IPv6 state, management
  profile, MTU, NetFlow profile, and comment.
- Finds the tunnel interface's virtual-router or logical-router/VRF and zone
  memberships without collapsing the two routing models into one scope.
- Reads template-inherited tunnel interfaces, logical-router memberships, and
  zones from effective-running configuration when SDK running-config discovery
  omits them.
- Reads the IPsec crypto profile, tunnel-monitor settings, access routes, client
  IP pools, and DNS settings available on the hub gateway.

## Standard IPsec migration plan

For each discovered satellite tunnel, the tool creates an in-memory plan and
prints:

- A generic spoke tunnel and IKE gateway name shared by compatible spoke
  firewalls, plus readable hub endpoint names containing both hostnames and a
  deterministic three-digit collision suffix.
- Reusable spoke interface/address and tunnel-interface values.
- Configured, active, and portal-designated gateway information.
- A proposed peer address, preferring the confirmed portal gateway, then the
  active gateway, then the configured portal address.
- Reusable hub gateway and tunnel-interface details.
- Published routes and relevant tunnel behavior settings.
- A cryptographically random per-spoke pre-shared key represented in generated
  configuration through Panorama `pre-shared-key` variables.
- A moderate, interoperability-oriented IKEv2 profile using AES-256-CBC,
  SHA-256, Diffie-Hellman group 14, and an eight-hour lifetime.
- A moderate ESP profile using AES-256-CBC, SHA-256, PFS group 14, and a
  one-hour lifetime. The migration plan displays only the selected ESP profile;
  it does not display an unused AH alternative.
- A newly allocated, collision-free `tunnel.N` interface on each endpoint. The
  interfaces receive unique point-to-point addresses from the operator-supplied
  pool and copy safe MTU, management, NetFlow, IPv6, and zone/routing-scope
  properties from the LSVPN sources. Candidate links overlapping configured
  interface networks on either firewall are skipped.
- Spoke static routes for the hub access-route list, or a default route when the
  blank LSVPN access-route setting represents full tunneling.
- Hub static routes for explicit or derived satellite-published networks.
- Spoke route metrics equal to ten times the portal gateway routing priority,
  preserving primary/backup preference.
- IKEv2 route-based default any/any traffic selectors. Explicit proxy IDs are
  intentionally omitted for this PAN-OS-to-PAN-OS design.
- Existing tunnel monitoring settings, with the destination changed to the new
  peer tunnel address.
- BGP peers bound to the source tunnel, including peer AS, peer-group type,
  authentication, BFD, and legacy virtual-router timer settings. Shared hub
  tunnel peers are matched to the spoke tunnel address before cloning.
- BGP peer-group routing policy inheritance. Legacy virtual-router export rules
  that target the peer group are recorded, and logical-router address-family,
  filtering-profile/route-map, timer, authentication, and dampening references
  are retained on the staged peer group. This preserves policies that advertise
  loopback prefixes, including link-local `/32` routes, over direct tunnel-bound
  BGP sessions.
- BGP-over-loopback topologies in which OSPF on the LSVPN tunnel provides
  reachability between loopbacks and an existing multihop BGP session carries
  production routes. Reciprocal loopback peers are detected explicitly; the
  existing BGP sessions are preserved rather than duplicated or rebound, while
  the replacement OSPF adjacency restores their underlay reachability.
  Tunnel-bound BGP peers take precedence: if either endpoint binds BGP directly
  to the LSVPN tunnel, loopback-transit candidates are discarded and the direct
  BGP cloning workflow is used instead.
- Reciprocal BGP-over-loopback sessions are also detected when OSPF is absent.
  Existing loopback-sourced BGP sessions remain unchanged, while reciprocal
  `/32` or `/128` static host routes to the peer addresses are installed through
  the new tunnel interfaces. This replaces reachability installed implicitly by
  LSVPN without changing the BGP source addresses. Matching old per-spoke static
  routes are removed from both endpoints while shared hub LSVPN configuration is
  preserved; unrelated old-interface routes remain a blocking dependency.
  Candidate loading is blocked only if a peer address cannot be resolved to an
  IP host route.
- OSPF area/interface membership for legacy virtual routers and logical-router
  VRFs. LSVPN point-to-multipoint interfaces become point-to-point interfaces
  because each replacement tunnel has exactly one peer; cost, authentication,
  BFD, and timer references are retained where the routing model supports them.
- Suppression of LSVPN-derived static routes when matching dynamic-routing
  protocols are discovered on both endpoints.
- Warnings for address mismatches, missing peer data, LSVPN certificate reuse,
  and incomplete configuration data.

The CLI caches a discovered hub gateway source during a run when multiple spoke
satellites refer to the same gateway.

The plan is converted into paired `pan-os-python` objects for
the spoke and hub: IKE crypto profiles, IPsec crypto profiles, IKE gateways,
auto-key IPsec tunnels, tunnel interfaces, zones, legacy virtual routers or
logical-router VRFs, IPv4/IPv6 static routes, BGP peers, and OSPF interfaces.
These objects are staged for preview by default. With `--load-candidate`, they
are added or updated in their source-owning Panorama templates and the obsolete
satellite-side objects and bindings are removed from candidate configuration.

## Configuration and packaging

- Requires Python 3.12 or newer.
- Is packaged as `panos-tunnel-builder` version `0.1.0`.
- Provides both the installed `panos-tunnel-builder` console command and the
  repository-level `main.py` launcher.
- Uses `pan-os-python`, `Typer`, and `python-dotenv`.
- Provides a `dev` dependency set containing pytest, pytest-cov, Ruff, mypy, and
  Pyright.
- Configures Pyright/Pylance in `pyproject.toml` for Python 3.12, the local
  `.venv`, and analysis of both `src` and `tests`.
- Loads these environment variables (including from a `.env` file):
  - `PANORAMA_HOST` (required)
  - `PANORAMA_API_KEY` (required)
  - `PANORAMA_PORT` (optional, defaults to `443`)
  - `PANORAMA_VERIFY_SSL` (optional, defaults to `true`)

## Error handling and safeguards

- Panorama API errors exit with status 1 and are labeled as API errors.
- Discovery and LSVPN ambiguity/configuration errors exit with status 1.
- Unexpected errors, including missing environment settings, exit with status 2.
- Interactive selections can be cancelled with menu option 0.
- Candidate writes require the explicit `--load-candidate` option and a second
  confirmation unless `--yes` is supplied.
- Candidate loading refuses unresolved requirements and unrelated name
  collisions before saving or writing migration configuration.
- Before the rollback snapshot or any write, candidate loading audits the full
  spoke template stack for references to the old LSVPN tunnel interface. Known
  satellite, interface, zone, routing membership, detected BGP, and detected
  OSPF references are handled; any other reference blocks the migration and
  reports its template and XML path.
- After all candidate writes and spoke cleanup, the tool rereads the generated
  objects and cleanup paths. It fails if an expected object/value is absent, an
  old spoke reference remains, or the hub GlobalProtect/LSVPN configuration
  fingerprint changed.
- Treats `vsys/import/network/interface` membership as a supported dependency:
  replacement hub and spoke tunnel interfaces are imported into their selected
  vsys, the old spoke tunnel import is removed, and post-write verification
  checks both new imports.
- Automatically issues Panorama's candidate rollback command when any candidate
  write, spoke cleanup, or post-write verification step fails. This requires no
  commit and does not load the saved snapshot, but it discards all pending
  candidate changes. A failed rollback is reported separately with an explicit
  partial-candidate warning, and the configuration lock is released either way.
- No code path commits Panorama or pushes configuration to a firewall.

## Not yet implemented

- Panorama commit, commit-all, or template-stack push. These operations remain
  intentionally operator-controlled.
- Automatic commit validation or post-commit firewall rollback. Candidate-load
  failures trigger a candidate rollback, but the tool does not commit the
  migration or issue firewall rollback operations after an operator commit.
- Generating or reviewing security policy rules required for the new VPN zones.
- OSPFv3 cloning; current OSPF discovery and staging covers IPv4 OSPFv2, while
  BGP supports IPv4 and IPv6 tunnel-link addressing.
- Automatically selecting among multiple portal gateways when neither active
  runtime state nor serial criteria identifies one candidate.
- Waiting for post-commit dynamic-routing convergence. The separate
  manifest-based verifier captures and enforces post-change route equivalence,
  but the operator decides when convergence is complete and starts that run.
- The greenfield workflow described in `projectdesc.readme`: when no LSVPN
  exists, discover external/untrust/internet-zone interfaces and build one or
  more new hub-and-spoke tunnels.

## Automated verification

At the time of this review:

- `pytest`: 135 tests passed with 83% total coverage.
- `ruff check .`: passed.
- `mypy src`: passed with no issues in 9 source files.
- `pyright`: passed with 0 errors and 0 warnings across `src` and `tests`.
- Read-only live route snapshots and independent-capture comparisons have been
  validated against both an advanced-routing logical-router firewall and
  `labPAN4` using the legacy virtual-router engine. The legacy validation also
  covers compact PAN-OS route flags such as `A?B`.
- The full candidate-only LSVPN replacement workflow has been validated live
  from `labPAN1` to `labPAN3` with `10.251.50.0/30`. Panorama reread verification
  passed, the old spoke satellite and `tunnel.20` were absent, both replacement
  endpoint object sets were present, and the hub LSVPN gateway remained intact.
  No commit or push was issued.
- Shared-template batching was validated live with `labPAN1` and `labPAN2`
  sharing `labBasicNetworkConfig` and `labPAN3` as the hub. A `10.251.50.0/29`
  pool supplied two `/30` links. Panorama accepted the shared spoke objects,
  per-device address/PSK overrides, and both serial-scoped hub endpoints;
  post-write verification passed with no commit or push.
- The same live candidate migration was run a second time. It updated the same
  objects, removed zero additional legacy bindings, preserved the complete PSK
  variable set, and passed post-write verification, confirming candidate-level
  idempotency. That historical validation predates the current safer rerun
  behavior, which rotates both ends of every PSK pair because encrypted Panorama
  values cannot prove that the stored plaintext still matches.

A GitHub Actions CI workflow runs on every push and pull request. It creates a
Python 3.12 virtual environment, installs the project with its development
dependencies, and requires pytest, Ruff, mypy, and Pyright to pass. The Pyright
check mirrors Pylance's standard type analysis so SDK typing regressions—such as
invalid inference for dynamically assigned `pan-os-python` attributes—are caught
outside the editor.

The tests use mocks and XML fixtures for Panorama behavior. They validate CLI
flow, template/firewall selection, LSVPN and gateway parsing, clone-plan output,
and major error cases; they are not live Panorama integration tests.
